{"id": "unknown-2026-07-11-013", "date": "2026-07-11", "time_tags": {"year": 2026, "quarter": "Q3", "month": 7, "week": 28, "weekday": "Sat"}, "entities": {"companies": [], "customers": [], "suppliers": []}, "topics": ["industry_general"], "sentiment": {"label": "neutral", "score": 0.0, "keywords": []}, "importance": {"score": 0.5, "reasons": []}, "title": "China could produce more batteries than global demand by 2030, says US think tank", "content": "<p>China’s battery cell manufacturing capacity could exceed projected global demand by 2030, according to a new Carnegie Endowment report, which recommends selective cooperation with Chinese companies to reduce supply chain risks. </p>\n<p>The post <a href=\"https://www.pv-magazine.com/2026/07/11/china-could-produce-more-batteries-than-global-demand-by-2030-says-us-think-tank/\">China could produce more batteries than global demand by 2030, says US think tank</a> appeared first on <a href=\"https://", "sources": [{"name": "pv_magazine", "url": "https://www.pv-magazine.com/2026/07/11/china-could-produce-more-batteries-than-global-demand-by-2030-says-us-think-tank/", "type": "rss"}]}
{"id": "unknown-2026-07-11-014", "date": "2026-07-11", "time_tags": {"year": 2026, "quarter": "Q3", "month": 7, "week": 28, "weekday": "Sat"}, "entities": {"companies": [], "customers": [], "suppliers": []}, "topics": ["industry_general"], "sentiment": {"label": "neutral", "score": 0.0, "keywords": []}, "importance": {"score": 0.5, "reasons": []}, "title": "End-of-life solar module recycling unviable if material diversity is not considered", "content": "<p>Researchers from Australia and Poland have collaborated on a study to determine the value of upcycling key materials from end-of-life panels such as aluminium, glass and solar cells with the express purpose of purifying the components for reuse in solar panel manufacturing.</p>\n<p>The post <a href=\"https://www.pv-magazine.com/2026/07/11/end-of-life-module-recycling-unviable-if-material-diversity-is-not-considered/\">End-of-life solar module recycling unviable if material diversity is not consi", "sources": [{"name": "pv_magazine", "url": "https://www.pv-magazine.com/2026/07/11/end-of-life-module-recycling-unviable-if-material-diversity-is-not-considered/", "type": "rss"}]}
{"id": "unknown-2026-07-11-015", "date": "2026-07-11", "time_tags": {"year": 2026, "quarter": "Q3", "month": 7, "week": 28, "weekday": "Sat"}, "entities": {"companies": [], "customers": [], "suppliers": []}, "topics": ["industry_general"], "sentiment": {"label": "neutral", "score": 0.0, "keywords": []}, "importance": {"score": 0.5, "reasons": []}, "title": "U.S. assembled solar module prices steady at $0.30/W", "content": "<p>U.S. solar module prices held steady at $0.30 per watt in the second quarter of 2026. The market saw a wave of corporate divestitures driven by federal trade rules. A new tariff petition against South Korean imports also impacted the industry.</p>\n<p>The post <a href=\"https://www.pv-magazine.com/2026/07/11/u-s-assembled-module-prices-steady-at-0-30-w/\">U.S. assembled solar module prices steady at $0.30/W</a> appeared first on <a href=\"https://www.pv-magazine.com\">pv magazine Global</a>.</p>\n", "sources": [{"name": "pv_magazine", "url": "https://www.pv-magazine.com/2026/07/11/u-s-assembled-module-prices-steady-at-0-30-w/", "type": "rss"}]}
{"id": "unknown-2026-07-11-016", "date": "2026-07-11", "time_tags": {"year": 2026, "quarter": "Q3", "month": 7, "week": 28, "weekday": "Sat"}, "entities": {"companies": [], "customers": [], "suppliers": []}, "topics": ["industry_general"], "sentiment": {"label": "neutral", "score": 0.0, "keywords": []}, "importance": {"score": 0.5, "reasons": []}, "title": "The Hydrogen Stream: Pilot project in Iceland shows geothermal-driven H2 could achieve LCOH of $1.75/kg", "content": "<p>Syntholene’s geothermal-integrated SOEC hydrogen platform could produce hydrogen at an estimated $1.75–$2.10/kg, about one-third the cost of comparable unsubsidized green hydrogen in Europe. Its Iceland demonstration facility aims to validate low-emission hydrogen production and support future commercial-scale deployment, while addressing risks such as electricity costs, electrolyzer durability, and capital expenses.</p>\n<p>The post <a href=\"https://www.pv-magazine.com/2026/07/11/the-hydrogen", "sources": [{"name": "pv_magazine", "url": "https://www.pv-magazine.com/2026/07/11/the-hydrogen-stream-pilot-project-in-iceland-shows-geothermal-driven-h2-could-achieve-lcoh-of-1-75-kg/", "type": "rss"}]}
